---
# This week note was published at .../2026-08-29-render-therefore-unto-ceaser/
# before the spelling of "Caesar" was fixed. This page keeps the old address
# working for anyone who bookmarked or linked it.
#
# GitHub Pages serves static files out of the folder the deploy workflow
# uploads and cannot issue a real 301 redirect: there is no .htaccess, no
# _redirects file, and jekyll-redirect-from is no help because Jekyll never
# runs here. So the old address answers 200 with this page, and the page sends
# the reader on. The canonical link is what tells a search engine the two
# addresses are one page, so links and search ranking follow the post.
permalink: /posts/weeknotes/2026-08-29-render-therefore-unto-ceaser/index.html
# Not a page of the site in its own right: keep it out of the feed, the
# sitemap, the notes list and the prev/next links.
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
movedTo: /posts/weeknotes/2026-08-29-render-therefore-unto-caesar/
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Render therefore unto Caesar – Ralph Hawkins</title>
  {#- The canonical link does the work for search engines. The two below do it
     for readers: replace() rather than assign() so Back does not bounce them
     forward into the redirect again — the same reasoning as src/index.njk —
     and the noscript refresh covers anything not running JavaScript. A zero
     delay is what makes the refresh a redirect rather than a timed one, which
     is the accessible way to write it. -#}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ site.url }}{{ movedTo }}">
  <script>
    window.location.replace("{{ movedTo }}");
  </script>
  <noscript><meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url={{ movedTo }}"></noscript>
</head>
<body>
  <p>This week note has moved to
    <a href="{{ movedTo }}">Render therefore unto Caesar</a>.</p>
</body>
</html>
